<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chaac</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chaak, Chac</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chaac is the Maya god of rain, lightning and storms, often shown with a long curling nose and an axe that strikes the clouds to bring rain. He was especially important in the dry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puuc">Puuc</a> region of Yucatán, where long-nosed masks traditionally identified with him cover the façades of buildings at Uxmal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabah">Kabah</a> and other sites. His cult survives in rain ceremonies in Yucatán today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chaac.jpg" alt="Chaac" width="235" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cultura Maya · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chac_c%C3%B3dice_maya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
